From 03fa9e8b5b7ff5062b5be046795b84a0fbc886c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: USA-RedDragon <USA-RedDragon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:43:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] fix(delegate): a chat message never stops its subagents'
 waits

A user's message to a chat interrupted the chat's whole spawn tree. A
subagent's process_manage wait was released with a note that promised a
notification on exit, which is false for a subagent's job, its model call was
cut, and its turn ended with the job still running and nobody watching it. The
subagent then sat as waiting for hours after the job had finished.

- A new-message interrupt (the reason a gateway names when the user sends a
  message to a busy chat) stops the chat's own turn only. Its subagents are
  steered explicitly; Stop and every other interrupt still reach them, and a
  child attached late no longer inherits a new-message interrupt.
- A yield for a parent's message is never aimed at a thread that runs one of
  its subagents.
- A subagent whose wait was released gets the job watched (as handoff with
  resume_on_exit does) and its note says so. A top-level agent keeps the
  notification promise.
- A subagent whose turn was cut short while a job of its own still runs has
  that job watched, so it is woken with the exit and shows as waiting only
  while the job runs. A job it keeps past its lifecycle is left alone.
